A40 — run this session's children directly, and through the linker when exec is
refused

Every binary in the payload lives in the app's data directory (filesDir), and the
modern host variant (targetSdk >= 29) may not exec a file there: W^X denies
untrusted_app -> execute_no_trans on app_data_file. The host works around it by
prefixing /system/bin/linker64 for every binary it starts, and gamescope spawns
its own children — the session reaper ("gamescopereaper", resolved through PATH)
and the game command after `--` — which had no such path and failed with
"Failed to start process ...: Permission denied" before the first frame.

Running them through the linker is right for the modern variant but wrong for the
legacy one, which may exec directly and whose wine depends on it: wine's loader
derives its library directory from /proc/self/exe, so behind the linker that
becomes the linker's path and wine dies at startup with

    wine: could not load ntdll.so: dlopen failed: library
    "/apex/com.android.runtime/bin/../lib/wine/aarch64-unix/ntdll.so" not found

So resolve argv[0] the way execvp would (a bare name is looked up along PATH,
since the linker needs an absolute path), exec it directly, and take the linker
only when the kernel answers EACCES. The modern variant behaves as before, the
legacy variant gets its direct exec back, and a failure still reports the real
error.

The two behaviours are one patch on purpose: as A40 (linker always) plus A42
(direct first) they edit the same lines, so with A42 applied A40 no longer
reverses, and the train reports PATCH DOES NOT APPLY on an already-patched tree.

--- a/src/Utils/Process.cpp
+++ b/src/Utils/Process.cpp
@@ -41,6 +41,109 @@
 
 static LogScope s_ProcessLog( "process" );
 
+#if defined( __ANDROID__ )
+// Android denies an app-domain process the right to exec a binary out of its
+// own data directory (W^X: untrusted_app -> execute_no_trans on app_data_file
+// is refused once targetSdk >= 29), which is why the host application starts
+// every binary through the system linker instead of exec'ing it. Children
+// gamescope spawns -- Xwayland and the game itself -- live in that same data
+// directory, so a plain execvp() on them fails with EACCES ("Failed to start
+// process ...: Permission denied"). Go through the linker the same way the host
+// does: linker64 <binary> <args...> loads the ELF with mmap(PROT_EXEC), which
+// is allowed.
+//
+// The linker needs an absolute path to the ELF, so a bare argv[0] (the reaper
+// is spawned as "gamescopereaper" and resolved through PATH) is looked up the
+// way execvp would have, and that resolved path becomes the program argument.
+static bool ResolveExecutablePath( const char *pszName, std::string &strOut )
+{
+    if ( pszName == nullptr || *pszName == '\0' )
+        return false;
+
+    if ( strchr( pszName, '/' ) != nullptr )
+    {
+        strOut = pszName;
+        return access( strOut.c_str(), X_OK ) == 0;
+    }
+
+    const char *pszPath = getenv( "PATH" );
+    if ( pszPath == nullptr )
+        return false;
+
+    std::string strDirs( pszPath );
+    size_t pos = 0;
+    while ( pos <= strDirs.size() )
+    {
+        size_t nSep = strDirs.find( ':', pos );
+        const std::string strDir = strDirs.substr( pos, nSep == std::string::npos ? std::string::npos : nSep - pos );
+
+        if ( !strDir.empty() )
+        {
+            std::string strCandidate = strDir + "/" + pszName;
+            if ( access( strCandidate.c_str(), X_OK ) == 0 )
+            {
+                strOut = std::move( strCandidate );
+                return true;
+            }
+        }
+
+        if ( nSep == std::string::npos )
+            break;
+        pos = nSep + 1;
+    }
+
+    return false;
+}
+
+[[noreturn]] static void RunChildExec( char *const argv[] )
+{
+    static constexpr const char *kLinker = "/system/bin/linker64";
+
+    // The plain exec comes first. A host that may exec its own data directory
+    // (the legacy variant, targetSdk < 29) gets the child started the way it
+    // starts everything else, which matters for wine: started through the
+    // linker, its /proc/self/exe is the linker's and it looks for ntdll.so
+    // beside that ("/apex/com.android.runtime/bin/../lib/wine") unless the host
+    // redirects the path, which only the modern variant does. The linker route
+    // is taken only when the kernel refuses the exec, which is the modern
+    // variant's W^X denial.
+    execvp( argv[0], argv );
+    if ( errno != EACCES )
+    {
+        s_ProcessLog.errorf_errno( "Failed to start process \"%s\"", argv[0] ? argv[0] : "(null)" );
+        _exit( 0 );
+    }
+
+    std::string strProgram;
+    if ( argv[0] != nullptr
+        && strcmp( argv[0], kLinker ) != 0
+        && ResolveExecutablePath( argv[0], strProgram ) )
+    {
+        std::vector<char *> linkerArgv;
+        linkerArgv.push_back( const_cast<char *>( kLinker ) );
+        linkerArgv.push_back( strProgram.data() );
+        for ( char *const *p = argv + 1; *p != nullptr; p++ )
+            linkerArgv.push_back( *p );
+        linkerArgv.push_back( nullptr );
+
+        execv( kLinker, linkerArgv.data() );
+    }
+
+    s_ProcessLog.errorf_errno( "Failed to start process \"%s\" through %s", argv[0] ? argv[0] : "(null)", kLinker );
+    _exit( 0 );
+}
+#else
+[[noreturn]] static void RunChildExec( char *const argv[] )
+{
+    if ( execvp( argv[0], argv ) == -1 )
+    {
+        s_ProcessLog.errorf_errno( "Failed to start process \"%s\"", argv[0] );
+    }
+    _exit( 0 );
+}
+#endif
+
